    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Falkenhayn planned to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> Heights so that the French would send their reserves to recapture them and suffer heavy losses; historians disagree whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> was his aim from the start or was put forward after the attack failed.</li><li>The battle lasted 302 days and was the longest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; Heer and Naumann calculated 377,231 French and 337,000 German casualties, while other historians give different totals.</li><li>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> came to stand for the determination of the army and the destructiveness of the war, and in 1984 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> stood together at the Douaumont cemetery.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Fort Douaumont, the largest fort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> region, held only a maintenance crew of about 68 technicians in February 1916, and a German party of about 100 soldiers entered it through an unmanned ditch bunker.</p></aside>
    </section>

