    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">Resources are taken from the forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, and the railway line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> is closed by the German 3rd Army in mid-July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1916</span><span class="rf-text">A bombardment by 808 guns opens Operation Judgement on 21 February and German infantry attack in the afternoon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1916</span><span class="rf-text">German troops enter Fort Douaumont, held by a small maintenance crew</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1916</span><span class="rf-text">The Germans extend the offensive to the west bank to destroy the French artillery</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Fort Vaux surrenders on 7 June, and the German advance of 23 June reaches Chapelle Sainte-Fine, its furthest point</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Falkenhayn orders the Germans onto the defensive after the failed attack on Fort Souville</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1916</span><span class="rf-text">French marines and colonial infantry recapture Fort Douaumont</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1916</span><span class="rf-text">A French offensive under Mangin takes 11,387 prisoners and recovers the ground lost in February</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-verdun" data-v="adc6dfd7f0" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The German guns open on the east bank</b> After delays from fog, rain and wind, a ten-hour bombardment began on the right bank, and the German corps attacked at 4:00 p.m. French XXX Corps held the north and north-east of the salient.</li><li><b>The Germans take Bois des Caures</b> German troops advanced about 5 km and took the wood after two French battalions had held it for two days. The French XXX Corps was forced out of its second line of defence.</li><li><b>A German party enters Fort Douaumont</b> Infantry reached their objectives in under twenty minutes, and two parties made for the fort. One of about 100 men found the ditch bunkers unmanned and took prisoner the small maintenance crew inside.</li><li><b>The attack widens to the west bank</b> Falkenhayn lent two divisions from the reserve, and the German VI and X Reserve Corps attacked towards Mort-Homme and Côte 304 to silence the French guns across the river. Those guns went on firing into the Germans on the east bank.</li><li><b>Fort Vaux is taken</b> The German attack shifted back to the right bank, south of Fort Douaumont. About 10,000 men assaulted Fort Vaux and the top was occupied on 2 June. Fighting continued underground until the garrison surrendered on 7 June.</li><li><b>The German salient towards Fort Souville</b> After a gas bombardment the Germans attacked on a front of 5 km and drove a salient of 3 by 2 km into the French defences. They came within 5 km of the Verdun citadel before the French recaptured Chapelle Sainte-Fine and the advance stopped.</li><li><b>The French offensives regain the ground</b> After a six-day bombardment French divisions attacked on 20 October and recaptured Fort Douaumont on 24 October. The Germans evacuated Fort Vaux on 2 November, and by 5 November the French were back at the front line of 24 February.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-the-marne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-the-marne.jpg" alt="First Battle of the Marne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-the-marne">First Battle of the Marne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_soldiers_Battle_of_Marne_WWI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">German Army, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the German advance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was halted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-the-marne">First Battle of the Marne</a> in 1914, offensives on the Western Front in 1914 and 1915 gained little ground and cost many casualties. According to his memoirs, the Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">German General Staff</a>, Erich von Falkenhayn, believed that victory might no longer come from a decisive battle but that the French army could be defeated if it suffered enough casualties. He offered five corps from the strategic reserve for an attack at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> only.</p><p>The plan rested on German heavy artillery, which had been used against Russian defences in the Gorlice–Tarnów Offensive of 1915 and had inflicted what are described as extraordinary casualties on the French in the Second Battle of Champagne. By taking or threatening Verdun, the Germans expected the French to send in their reserves to attack secure German positions, and the British to launch a relief offensive that would also fail. If France then refused to negotiate, a second phase would attack the weakened armies.</p><p>The Fortified Region of Verdun lay in a salient formed in 1914. It had a double ring of 28 forts and smaller works. General Joseph Joffre had concluded from the fall of the Belgian fortresses in 1914 that forts were obsolete, and in 1915 the region lost 54 artillery batteries. By 1916 the 18 large forts and other batteries had fewer than 300 guns, and their garrisons were small maintenance crews.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Verdun&#8221; cites where it covers Strategic developments, Background, German preparations, French preparations:</p><ul><li>C. Holstein, Fort Douaumont (Pen and Sword, 2010)</li><li>R. T. Foley, German Strategy and the Path to Verdun: Erich von Falkenhayn and the Development of Attrition, 1870–1916 (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li><li>R. A. Doughty, Pyrrhic victory: French Strategy and Operations in the Great War (The Belknap Press of Harvard University, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German 5th Army, under Crown Prince Wilhelm, divided the front into four areas and received the III Corps, VII Reserve Corps and XVIII Corps, each reinforced by 2,400 experienced troops and 2,000 trained recruits. Ten new rail lines were built, and shelters 4.5 to 14 metres deep were dug for up to 1,200 infantry each. A total of 1,201 guns were massed, two thirds of them heavy or super-heavy, taken from the rest of the Western Front, with ammunition for 2,000,000 rounds in the first six days.</p><p>The plan called for limited objectives: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> Heights on a line from Froide Terre to Fort Souville and Fort Tavannes. Artillery was to carry the burden and infantry casualties were to be kept low. The phrase relentless pressure, added by the 5th Army staff, left the purpose of the offensive unclear. Falkenhayn wanted ground from which artillery could dominate the field, while the 5th Army wanted a quick capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>.</p><p>On the French side General Frédéric-Georges Herr held the front with 8 and a half divisions in line and 2 and a half in close reserve. Joffre expected a diversion and had 25 divisions in the strategic reserve. French artillery reinforcements brought the total at Verdun to 388 field guns and 244 heavy guns. Joffre had been warned in January by accurate intelligence of German intentions but considered that an attack would be a diversion.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Verdun&#8221; cites where it covers German plan:</p><ul><li>R. T. Foley, German Strategy and the Path to Verdun: Erich von Falkenhayn and the Development of Attrition, 1870–1916 (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bad weather delayed the attack from 12 to 21 February, when a ten-hour bombardment by 808 guns began at 7:15 a.m. Infantry attacked at 4:00 p.m. with flamethrowers, followed by stormtroopers using hand grenades, and the Germans suffered only about 600 casualties. By 22 February they had advanced 5 kilometres, and Colonel Émile Driant was killed at Bois des Caures. On 25 February a German party of about 100 soldiers entered Fort Douaumont through an unmanned ditch bunker and took Warrant Officer Chenot and about 25 men prisoner. Pétain took command that night.</p><p>The advance slowed on 27 February, after a thaw and the arrival of French reinforcements. In March the Germans extended the attack to the west bank, towards Mort-Homme and Côte 304, to destroy the French guns there. The attacks cost many casualties, and by the end of March the offensive had cost the Germans 81,607. Falkenhayn began to think of ending the offensive, but the 5th Army staff wanted to go on, and by the end of April he had agreed to wide-front attacks.</p><p>The French retook part of Fort Douaumont on 22 May but were cut off and surrendered, 1,000 prisoners being taken. Fort Vaux was taken after a final assault on 1 June, and its 574 survivors surrendered on 7 June. On 23 June the Germans, after a gas bombardment, advanced to Chapelle Sainte-Fine, within about 5 kilometres of the citadel. An attack on Fort Souville failed on 11 and 12 July. In October and December French counter-offensives recovered Douaumont, Vaux and most of the lost ground.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Verdun&#8221; cites where it covers First phase, 21 February – 1 March, Second phase, 6 March – 15 April, Third phase, 16 April – 1 July, Fourth phase 1 July – 17 December:</p><ul><li>C. Holstein, Fort Douaumont (Pen and Sword, 2010)</li><li>R. T. Foley, German Strategy and the Path to Verdun: Erich von Falkenhayn and the Development of Attrition, 1870–1916 (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li><li>R. A. Doughty, Pyrrhic victory: French Strategy and Operations in the Great War (The Belknap Press of Harvard University, 2005)</li><li>G. C. Wynne, If Germany Attacks: The Battle in Depth in the West (Faber &amp; Faber, 1976)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg.jpg" alt="Paul von Hindenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-C06886,_Paul_v._Hindenburg_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author., CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The French recaptured Fort Douaumont on 24 October, after a six-day bombardment of 855,264 shells, and took more than 6,000 prisoners and fifteen guns by 25 October. The Germans evacuated Fort Vaux on 2 November, and the French reached the front line of 24 February on 5 November. The second offensive began on 15 December after a bombardment of 1,169,000 shells. Of 21,000 men in the five German front divisions, 13,500 were lost, most of them taken prisoner. The French took 11,387 prisoners and 115 guns and pushed the closest German point 7.5 kilometres back.</p><p>Falkenhayn was replaced on 29 August by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a>, who ended the German offensive on 2 September. The general of the 5th Army and a corps commander were sacked on 16 December. The offensives on the Somme and by Brusilov had already forced the Germans to move troops and guns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, and the Germans were limited to small local attacks after 12 July.</p><p>Historians give many casualty totals. Heer and Naumann calculated 377,231 French and 337,000 German, 714,231 in all. Clayton gave 351,000 French, of whom 56,000 were killed, 100,000 missing or prisoners and 195,000 wounded, and 330,000 German, of whom 143,000 were killed or missing. Philpott recorded 377,000 French, 162,000 of them killed, and 337,000 German. The battlefield contains the remains of over 100,000 missing soldiers.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Verdun&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Morale, Subsequent operations:</p><ul><li>P. Jankowski, Verdun: The Longest Battle of the Great War (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li><li>R. A. Doughty, Pyrrhic victory: French Strategy and Operations in the Great War (The Belknap Press of Harvard University, 2005)</li><li>Elizabeth Greenhalgh, The French Army and the First World War (Cambridge University Press, 2014)</li><li>R. T. Foley, German Strategy and the Path to Verdun: Erich von Falkenhayn and the Development of Attrition, 1870–1916 (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="Winston Churchill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Winston_Churchill_-_19086236948_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Casualty figures for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> are hard to fix because the records were kept in different ways. Paul Jankowski described how French units produced numerical loss states every five days, while the German armies compiled Verlustlisten every ten days, and medical data was published separately. These records counted losses over time, not by place. Some French data excluded the lightly wounded and some did not, and the German Verlustlisten excluded them. Churchill added 2 per cent to the German figures for unrecorded wounded, and the British official historian James Edmonds added 30 per cent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> in 1927 gave 442,000 French casualties; Robert Doughty wrote that he and those who followed him had mistakenly included all French losses on the Western Front. In the second edition of The World Crisis in 1938, Churchill gave 278,000 German casualties and deducted an eighth from his figures for other sectors. Terraine, Dupuy and Dupuy, Mason, Grant, Foley and Afflerbach each give figures that differ from these.</p><p>The aims of Falkenhayn are also disputed. His memoirs quote a memorandum of December 1915 saying that the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> would bleed to death. Holger Afflerbach concluded in 1994 that it was written after the war but reflected his thinking at the end of 1915, while Gerd Krumeich wrote that it was fabricated to justify a failed strategy. Robert Foley wrote in 2007 that Falkenhayn intended a battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> from the beginning, as against the views of Wolfgang Foerster and Krumeich, noting that the loss of documents led to many interpretations.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Verdun&#8221; cites where it covers Analysis:</p><ul><li>R. T. Foley, German Strategy and the Path to Verdun: Erich von Falkenhayn and the Development of Attrition, 1870–1916 (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li><li>P. Jankowski, Verdun: The Longest Battle of the Great War (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the battle came to symbolise the determination of the French army and the destructiveness of the war. It became for the French the representative memory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, as the Somme is for the United Kingdom. Antoine Prost wrote that like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> marks a transgression of the limits of the human condition. Between 1918 and 1939 the French held two memories of it: a patriotic one in the memorials on the battlefield and in Nivelle's words, They shall not pass, and the survivors' memory of death and suffering.</p><p>In 1920 a ceremony in the citadel of Verdun chose the body for the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier at the Arc de Triomphe. Six villages were not rebuilt and became uninhabited communes. Forests planted in the 1930s hide most of the Zone rouge, but the battlefield remains a vast graveyard. Mortal remains found by the French Forestry Service are laid in the Douaumont ossuary.</p><p>German commanders of the 5th Army tried to capture Verdun and attacked regardless of loss, and the offensive lasted far longer and took far more infantry than Falkenhayn had intended. French morale suffered: five infantry regiments saw collective indiscipline in 1916, and Lieutenants Henri Herduin and Pierre Millant were shot on 11 June and exonerated after an inquiry in 1926. In the 1960s Verdun became a symbol of Franco-German reconciliation, and in the 1980s a capital of peace. On 22 September 1984 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> stood together at the Douaumont cemetery.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Verdun&#8221; cites where it covers Commemoration:</p><ul><li>R. T. Foley, German Strategy and the Path to Verdun: Erich von Falkenhayn and the Development of Attrition, 1870–1916 (Cambridge University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Battle of Verdun lasted from 21 February to 18 December 1916 and was the longest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The German attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> Heights did not gain the position it aimed for, and French counter-offensives recovered most of the ground. Casualties are put at about 714,000.</em></p>

